# A binding of a name the Heroes runtime's own headers declare or define,
# refused at `build` whatever header its group names (design.md §4.19, §4.20,
# §1.12; panel 232's R6, provisional, author ratification pending; defects
# 2256, 2026-10-29, and 2876, 2026-11-27).
#
# Every unit reads `heroes_unit.h`, and so `heroes_runtime.h`, before its
# groups' headers, so a group binding a function of the runtime's finds it
# declared whatever header it names. `function hero_str_release_at(@s: str)`
# bound under `extern "stdio.h"`, `"math.h"`, `"hero_os.h"` (which includes
# the runtime's header), `"heroes_unit.h"`, `"./heroes_runtime.h"` or a
# header of the program's own that includes it, each built, freed a string
# the program still held, and ended at 134 on the runtime's mark check
# (measured 2026-10-29, the shapes beside the defect's own). The group that
# names `heroes_runtime.h` itself is refused at `check`
# (`check/runtime_header.hero`); this is the rest of that door, told at
# `build`, which knows the runtime it compiles against and reads its header.
#
# **The runtime's names are screened by the words its two first headers' C
# writes in the compiler's own forms** (`own_names.reserved`: `h_`, `hero_`,
# `Hero`, `HERO_`, `HEROES_`, `main`), their comments and literals passed
# over (`c_words.used`), forms panel 214's R4 holds every name and macro of
# the runtime's headers to (`cli/own_names.hero`'s test). A word of another
# form a header writes, a parameter's `text` or C's own `int64_t`, is no
# binding's business, and a third party's `hero_jump` the runtime does not
# write binds as before. `hero_os.h`'s own names are not the header's, so a
# program binds them as the library does.
#
# **Each screened name is told as what it is, and by the header that gives
# it** (defect 2876): until then every word was told as a function
# `heroes_runtime.h` declares, `HERO_STANDARD_AFTER_GROUPS` among them, a
# macro `heroes_unit.h` defines and the runtime's header only tests, and a
# member's name, `hero_code`, was refused where a program's own header
# declared a function of it. `cli/runtime_kinds.hero` says what a word is: a
# macro either header defines, or a function, variable, type or enumeration
# constant clang reads the two declaring at the file's scope, each refused
# and told so; a member's, a parameter's or a word an `#if` only tests is no
# name of the runtime's, and its binding is told as any other's is.
#
# **The switch is refused, measured** (that day, the unit's C for the
# binding written by hand, with the line the guard adds for a bound name of
# its list): bound under `stdio.h`, `heroes_unit.h` or a header of the
# program's own that defines nothing, its accessor read `1`, the compiler's
# switch, found only because the unit reads `heroes_unit.h` first, where spec
# § 13 holds a group's constant to the header it names; under a header that
# defines it again as `7`, it read `7`. A program reads the value the
# compiler set unless its own header undoes the compiler's switch, so the
# name is the runtime's as its other names are.

use ast
use check/runtime_header
use cli/compile
use cli/own_names
use cli/runtime_kinds
use cli/strings
use diag
use module/reading
use source
use token

# Why no program binds a name of the runtime's, said of every kind but a
# function.
constant WHY_NAME: str
    "they are the C this compiler writes for itself, which another compiler may change or take away"

# Why no program binds a function of the runtime's.
constant WHY_FUNCTION: str
    "the runtime's functions count the references of the program's own values and free them, so one called from Heroes can free what the program still holds"

# What the program binds instead, said of every kind but a function, whose
# note is `runtime_header.INSTEAD`.
constant INSTEAD_NAME: str
    "what the runtime offers a program is `hero_os.h`'s, threads, processes and files, and a constant or a type of the C library is its own header's, `stdint.h` or `limits.h`: name the header that declares the name the program needs"

# The build stopped where a binding names the runtime's own: its refusals
# printed as `check`'s are, and the failure `compile.leave` gives a stage.
function stopped(s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], build: str, runtime: str, permissive: bool) -> ()?
    stop = compile.stage(told(s, decls, build: build, runtime: runtime), s, permissive: permissive)

    if stop.is_err()
        return ok()
    _ = compile.leave(stop.must(), stage_name: "the runtime's own names")?
    return ok()

# Each binding of the runtime's own names, told at the name, in the order the
# declarations stand; none where the runtime's header cannot be read now,
# which the toolchain read before any unit was written (`runtime_places`).
# clang is asked, under `build`, only where a binding of the compiler's forms
# names a word the headers' code writes and no `#define` of theirs names.
function told(s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], build: str, runtime: str) -> [diag.Diagnostic]
    out: [diag.Diagnostic] @= []
    formed: [token.Span] @= []

    for decl in decls
        named = bound(decl)

        if !named.is_err() && own_names.reserved(source.span_text(s, named.must()))
            formed @ formed.push(named.must())

    # A program binding no name of the compiler's forms reads no header here.
    if formed.len() == 0
        return out
    read = reading.source_text(runtime + "/heroes_runtime.h")

    if read.is_err()
        return out
    unit = reading.source_text(runtime + "/heroes_unit.h")

    unit_text = if unit.is_err()
        ""
    else
        unit.must().text
    p = runtime_kinds.prefix_of(unit: unit_text, runtime: read.must().text)
    screened: [token.Span] @= []
    asks: bool @= false

    for span in formed
        name = source.span_text(s, span)

        if runtime_kinds.written(p, name: name)
            screened @ screened.push(span)
            asks @ asks || !runtime_kinds.a_macro(p, name: name)

    declared: {str: str} @= {}
    answered: bool @= !asks

    if asks
        answer = runtime_kinds.asked(build: build, runtime: runtime)

        if !answer.is_err()
            declared @ answer.must()
            answered @ true

    for span in screened
        name = source.span_text(s, span)
        o = runtime_kinds.owned(p, name: name, declared: declared, answered: answered)

        if !o.is_err()
            out @ out.push(diagnostic(name: name, span: span, o: o.must()))

    return out

# The refusal of `name`, said as what `o` says it is and the header that
# gives it.
function diagnostic(name: str, span: token.Span, o: runtime_kinds.Owned) -> diag.Diagnostic
    function_kind = o.what == runtime_kinds.FUNCTION

    why = if function_kind
        WHY_FUNCTION
    else if o.what == runtime_kinds.WRITTEN
        WHY_NAME + ", and its functions count the references of the program's own values and free them"
    else
        WHY_NAME
    d: diag.Diagnostic @= diag.new_diagnostic(
        code: runtime_header.CODE,
        message: "`" + name + "` is the Heroes runtime's own, " + o.what + " its `" + o.file + "` " + runtime_kinds.verb(o) + ", which every unit reads before its groups' headers whatever header a group names, and no program binds the runtime's own names: " + why,
        span: span
    )

    note = if function_kind
        runtime_header.INSTEAD
    else
        INSTEAD_NAME
    d.notes @ d.notes.push(note)
    return d

# The C name a group's declaration binds, where it stands in the program: a
# function's or a constant's name, a record's tag where it gives one and its
# name where not; none for a declaration of no group.
function bound(decl: ast.Decl) -> token.Span?
    match decl.kind
        .function_decl fd =>
            if !fd.value.header.is_err()
                return ok(decl.name)
        .constant_decl k =>
            if !k.header.is_err()
                return ok(decl.name)
        .record_decl rec =>
            if !rec.header.is_err()
                if !rec.tag.is_err()
                    return rec.tag
                return ok(decl.name)
        .variant_decl | .test_decl => return fail(code: "none", msg: "no group")

    return fail(code: "none", msg: "no group")

## Tests

test "a name of the runtime's is told as what it is and by the header that gives it, a function alone as one that frees (defect 2876)"
    span = token.Span(start: 0, end: 1)
    unit_switch = diagnostic(
        name: "HERO_STANDARD_AFTER_GROUPS",
        span: span,
        o: runtime_kinds.Owned(what: runtime_kinds.MACRO, file: runtime_kinds.UNIT)
    )
    assert strings.starts_with(
        text: unit_switch.message,
        prefix: "`HERO_STANDARD_AFTER_GROUPS` is the Heroes runtime's own, a macro its `heroes_unit.h` defines, which every unit reads"
    )
    assert !strings.holds(text: unit_switch.message, needle: "free") && unit_switch.notes == [INSTEAD_NAME]
    function_d = diagnostic(
        name: "hero_print_int",
        span: span,
        o: runtime_kinds.Owned(what: runtime_kinds.FUNCTION, file: runtime_kinds.RUNTIME)
    )
    assert strings.starts_with(
        text: function_d.message,
        prefix: "`hero_print_int` is the Heroes runtime's own, a function its `heroes_runtime.h` declares,"
    )
    assert strings.holds(text: function_d.message, needle: WHY_FUNCTION) && function_d.notes == [runtime_header.INSTEAD]
    constant_d = diagnostic(
        name: "HERO_DESC_EQ_AT",
        span: span,
        o: runtime_kinds.Owned(what: runtime_kinds.ENUMERATOR, file: runtime_kinds.RUNTIME)
    )
    assert strings.starts_with(
        text: constant_d.message,
        prefix: "`HERO_DESC_EQ_AT` is the Heroes runtime's own, an enumeration constant its `heroes_runtime.h` declares,"
    )
